# Project Index

`.project-index.yaml` at the repository root maps modules, key files, tags and summaries so agents can locate relevant files without a full-tree search.

The index is optional. When it is absent, this rule does not apply: you MUST NOT create one as a side effect of another task. The `project-index` skill builds it when the user wants one.

## Consult

- when locating files and the index exists, you MUST read it before `fd`, glob, or a repository-wide `rg`
- you SHOULD read the top-level `summary` first, then match the task to module `summary` and `tags`, then open the matching `key_files`
- you MAY widen the search with `fd` / `rg` (see `core.mdc` Extra Tools) at any time
- when `generated_at_commit` does not match `HEAD`, the index may lag the working tree: you MUST NOT treat a miss as authoritative, and MUST widen the search instead
- `git diff --name-only <generated_at_commit>..HEAD` tells you whether that drift touched the paths you care about; you SHOULD run it before trusting an index that lags
- the index is a map, not a spec: you MUST confirm behavior in the source you open
- you MUST NOT invent modules or files that are absent from both the index and the working tree

## Maintain

- when you add, remove, rename, or change the role of a file the index covers, you MUST update the affected module entries in the same change
- you MUST keep the file's existing shape: sorted entries, one-line summaries, reusable lowercase tags
- you MUST NOT copy secrets or large code excerpts into the index
- for more than incremental upkeep — a first index, or a rebuild after a refactor — you SHOULD use the `project-index` skill

`style-ponytail.mdc` outranks this rule: you MUST NOT grow a small change into a large index rewrite.
